WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.421 --> 00:00:13.385
The economic cost alone
of the loss of nature and biodiversity

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:18.974
arising out of the unabated release
of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:23.186
is estimated at about 44 trillion dollars.

00:00:24.604 --> 00:00:28.191
We need to reimagine
the way the world produces,

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:31.361
consumes and prices energy,

00:00:32.195 --> 00:00:33.697
and we need to do it now.

00:00:35.073 --> 00:00:39.494
And so I'm here to tell you a love story.

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:44.749
The lovers in this story
might seem mismatched,

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:47.586
but their love has the power
to change the world

00:00:47.627 --> 00:00:50.171
by reimagining the global energy system.

00:00:51.381 --> 00:00:54.175
It's a story we all need to understand.

00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:57.804
A story with a complicated meet cute,

00:00:57.846 --> 00:01:00.056
but ultimately a simple resolution.

00:01:01.182 --> 00:01:05.061
It's the story of an electron
and a water molecule.

00:01:06.605 --> 00:01:11.693
Now in order to truly empathize
with my electron's narrative arc,

00:01:11.693 --> 00:01:13.737
you need to know a bit about her,

00:01:13.737 --> 00:01:16.239
her history, her personality.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.869
Electrons have powered the modern world
since the 19th century,

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:24.998
when scientists discovered
that using magnets and coils of wire,

00:01:24.998 --> 00:01:29.753
they could turn a flow of motion
into electrons carrying energy.

00:01:31.338 --> 00:01:36.384
That was just the beginning
of our increasingly electrified existence.

00:01:36.426 --> 00:01:38.303
Behind almost everything we do,

00:01:38.345 --> 00:01:41.222
there are supply chains,
refrigeration systems,

00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:44.309
data centers and much, much more.

00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:48.396
And so it's perhaps not surprising

00:01:48.438 --> 00:01:51.566
that the global production
of electricity alone

00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:56.780
constitutes about 28 percent
of total emissions of carbon dioxide,

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.365
because for much of that time,

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:01.576
electricity has been produced
by burning fossil fuels,

00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:03.995
which emit carbon dioxide as a byproduct.

00:02:06.748 --> 00:02:09.209
But not our heroine.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:11.294
She's a green electron.

00:02:11.336 --> 00:02:14.464
Her energy comes from the sun,
the wind, the tide.

00:02:14.506 --> 00:02:16.383
An unlimited renewable resource

00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:22.389
which produces electricity
without emitting any carbon dioxide.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.265
And there is good news here.

00:02:24.307 --> 00:02:28.603
The share of green electrons
in the global electricity system

00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:30.855
is approximately 30 percent,

00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:33.358
and it's rising rapidly.

00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:38.029
So in that case,

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:40.657
addressing climate change should be easy.

00:02:40.657 --> 00:02:43.618
We just need to electrify everything

00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:48.039
and make sure that we have enough
green electrons like her to power it.

00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:51.459
No.

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:55.213
I identify very strongly
with the green electron,

00:02:55.255 --> 00:02:58.383
perhaps because I've spent
virtually all my professional life

00:02:58.425 --> 00:03:01.678
working to deliver her
at the greatest possible volume

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.097
and the lowest possible cost.

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:09.102
But our green electron
can't save the world on her own.

00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:13.815
And that's why this is a love story
and not a coming-of-age narrative.

00:03:14.607 --> 00:03:16.776
(Laughter)

00:03:16.776 --> 00:03:18.945
(Applause)

00:03:23.491 --> 00:03:26.828
Electrons can't save the world alone. Yep.

00:03:26.828 --> 00:03:28.747
The world also needs molecules.

00:03:28.747 --> 00:03:33.084
Now I think of molecules,
if not as exactly opposite to electrons,

00:03:33.084 --> 00:03:35.253
certainly as complementary.

00:03:35.295 --> 00:03:38.381
If I were to ascribe
a personality to a molecule,

00:03:38.423 --> 00:03:40.341
it would be consistent,

00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:42.969
reliable, self-contained.

00:03:43.678 --> 00:03:45.013
Unlike electrons,

00:03:45.055 --> 00:03:51.311
molecules are excellent at storing
and releasing energy efficiently.

00:03:51.352 --> 00:03:53.730
They're critical for industrial processes

00:03:53.772 --> 00:03:57.776
which need the intense heat
which comes from burning of fuel.

00:03:57.776 --> 00:04:01.112
They're essential for long-distance
shipping and aviation,

00:04:01.112 --> 00:04:05.366
which need to store, transport
and release energy efficiently.

00:04:06.576 --> 00:04:10.622
But these sectors, which are critical
to the global economy,

00:04:10.622 --> 00:04:13.541
they can't be electrified efficiently.

00:04:14.292 --> 00:04:17.045
And these hard-to-electrify sectors,

00:04:17.087 --> 00:04:22.675
they currently equate to about 30 percent
of total annual carbon dioxide emissions

00:04:22.717 --> 00:04:25.804
because virtually all their molecules
come from fossil fuels.

00:04:27.388 --> 00:04:30.725
And until recently
there wasn't a viable alternative

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:33.895
to obtaining those molecules
from fossil fuels.

00:04:33.937 --> 00:04:36.898
And so no credible route to net zero,

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.276
no possibility of reimagining
the global energy system,

00:04:40.276 --> 00:04:42.529
no world that runs
entirely on green energy.

00:04:43.738 --> 00:04:44.989
But …

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.535
here enters our second lover.

00:04:48.535 --> 00:04:50.870
Our molecule of H2O.

00:04:51.871 --> 00:04:53.123
Water.

00:04:54.332 --> 00:04:57.418
Not a traditional pairing
with electricity,

00:04:58.628 --> 00:05:01.256
but as Emily Dickinson reminds us,

00:05:01.297 --> 00:05:03.174
the heart wants what it wants.

00:05:03.216 --> 00:05:04.759
(Laughter)

00:05:07.095 --> 00:05:10.557
And so our love story goes like this.

00:05:10.598 --> 00:05:14.269
The green electron
and the water molecule meet.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:18.231
They feel a spark. They fall in love.

00:05:18.273 --> 00:05:22.026
And from that love
is born a green molecule.

00:05:22.026 --> 00:05:25.238
A molecule which can
store and release energy

00:05:25.238 --> 00:05:28.241
without any fossil fuels getting involved.

00:05:29.284 --> 00:05:32.078
Or, if you prefer the scientific version:

00:05:33.288 --> 00:05:34.706
using electrolysis,

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:39.669
the power of green electrons can be used
to split water into its constituent parts,

00:05:39.711 --> 00:05:41.796
oxygen and hydrogen,

00:05:41.838 --> 00:05:44.340
leaving us with hydrogen, H2.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.095
Hydrogen is an extremely flexible fuel.

00:05:49.137 --> 00:05:51.389
It can be burnt to provide energy

00:05:51.431 --> 00:05:54.642
for heat-intensive processes
like steel production.

00:05:54.684 --> 00:05:56.936
It can be synthesized
with biogenic carbon,

00:05:56.978 --> 00:05:58.980
nonfossil-based carbon,

00:05:59.022 --> 00:06:00.273
to produce methanol,

00:06:00.315 --> 00:06:04.194
and that methanol can be used
to decarbonize the chemical industry

00:06:04.194 --> 00:06:07.363
or as a fuel for long-distance shipping.

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:12.327
That methanol can be further synthesized
into e-kerosene, jet fuel,

00:06:12.327 --> 00:06:16.581
which raises the possibility
of emissions-free long-distance aviation.

00:06:16.581 --> 00:06:22.629
Or finally, hydrogen can be synthesized
with nitrogen to produce ammonia.

00:06:24.589 --> 00:06:29.010
So now surely I'm going to tell you
that they all lived happily ever after,

00:06:29.052 --> 00:06:30.887
like all the great love stories,

00:06:30.929 --> 00:06:33.473
in a world that runs
entirely on green energy.

00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:36.017
But no.

00:06:37.352 --> 00:06:40.396
Because at the current rate of progress,

00:06:40.438 --> 00:06:43.316
of deploying, scaling up
these technologies,

00:06:43.358 --> 00:06:48.404
we are absolutely not on track to achieve
a one-and-a-half-degree pathway.

00:06:50.323 --> 00:06:53.576
But we are still writing this story.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:58.957
We know that industry can scale up
these breakthrough technologies

00:06:58.957 --> 00:07:00.416
very rapidly

00:07:00.416 --> 00:07:02.961
because at Ørsted, 
we’ve done this before.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:09.008
In 1991, we installed
the world's first offshore wind turbine,

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:12.387
so close to shore
you could swim around it.

00:07:12.428 --> 00:07:14.597
Back then, offshore wind was expensive.

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:19.185
It was widely considered
to be uneconomically viable.

00:07:19.227 --> 00:07:21.312
It was based on immature technology.

00:07:21.354 --> 00:07:24.816
There was a lot of doubt
about its long-term viability.

00:07:25.608 --> 00:07:27.443
But we committed to bring down costs.

00:07:27.443 --> 00:07:29.654
We optimized every component.

00:07:29.654 --> 00:07:31.322
We worked with the supply chain

00:07:31.322 --> 00:07:34.993
so that they could invest
in their manufacturing capacity.

00:07:34.993 --> 00:07:40.039
And as the European policymakers
saw that we and the industry,

00:07:40.039 --> 00:07:43.751
the progress that we were making
on cost and on efficiency,

00:07:43.793 --> 00:07:47.213
they reimagined
the European electricity system

00:07:47.255 --> 00:07:50.967
to put offshore wind
as a critical pillar of it.

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:53.011
And the US and Asian markets --

00:07:53.052 --> 00:07:57.932
like Japan, Korea, Taiwan
and of course China -- have followed.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.023
And now we need to take
the green molecule on the same journey,

00:08:05.064 --> 00:08:06.899
but much faster.

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:13.531
In May, we broke ground
on Europe’s largest e-methanol plant,

00:08:13.531 --> 00:08:15.158
in northern Sweden.

00:08:15.158 --> 00:08:17.535
In 2025, when it's operational,

00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:19.871
it will produce enough green methanol

00:08:19.871 --> 00:08:24.417
to power one oceangoing container vessel.

00:08:25.251 --> 00:08:28.588
We’re already planning the next,
five to eight times the size.

00:08:28.629 --> 00:08:32.133
In the US, on the Gulf Coast
and all over the world,

00:08:32.175 --> 00:08:34.802
developers like us
are working with companies

00:08:34.844 --> 00:08:39.307
who want to defossilize their operations
to build green hydrogen projects.

00:08:41.642 --> 00:08:47.982
But the global energy system
cannot be defossilized by individuals,

00:08:48.024 --> 00:08:49.400
even those in love.

00:08:50.109 --> 00:08:53.404
Not by individual projects,
no matter their scale,

00:08:53.404 --> 00:08:55.198
not by individual companies.

00:08:55.198 --> 00:08:58.284
Even whole countries acting alone

00:08:58.284 --> 00:09:02.038
cannot transform the global energy system

00:09:02.038 --> 00:09:05.375
away from one which
is dependent on fossil fuels.

00:09:08.836 --> 00:09:13.841
Until now, the price of fossil fuels

00:09:13.883 --> 00:09:16.803
has not reflected the true cost

00:09:16.844 --> 00:09:20.765
of the unabated release
of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.

00:09:20.807 --> 00:09:25.728
It does not reflect the costs
of adaptation and mitigation

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.563
to a changing climate.

00:09:28.856 --> 00:09:32.235
And so those costs,
they fall on all of us.

00:09:32.985 --> 00:09:36.906
Inevitably, they fall on us inequitably.

00:09:38.449 --> 00:09:39.951
And that must change.

00:09:41.369 --> 00:09:44.622
Too often, the costs of defossilization,

00:09:44.622 --> 00:09:48.751
whether of companies, cities, countries,

00:09:48.793 --> 00:09:51.045
is dismissed as too expensive.

00:09:52.171 --> 00:09:57.718
And yet, if the price of fossil fuels
reflected their true cost,

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:03.891
the economic imperative would be
to seek out the defossilized version.

00:10:03.933 --> 00:10:06.310
The economic imperative would be

00:10:06.352 --> 00:10:11.941
to scale up these solutions
we already have at hand.

00:10:12.859 --> 00:10:16.195
A world which runs entirely
on green energy,

00:10:16.195 --> 00:10:18.281
it's not a luxury,

00:10:18.281 --> 00:10:19.824
it's a necessity.

00:10:19.824 --> 00:10:25.621
(Applause)

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:31.210
And so to come back,
finally, to my love story.

00:10:31.961 --> 00:10:34.714
In the UK, where I'm from,

00:10:34.755 --> 00:10:37.133
there's a moment in the wedding ceremony

00:10:37.175 --> 00:10:39.886
which recognizes the importance
of communal action.

00:10:41.137 --> 00:10:44.348
It’s my favorite moment every time,

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:50.354
because it reflects the fact
that marriage is an intensely private act.

00:10:50.396 --> 00:10:53.274
But families, families of all kinds,

00:10:54.484 --> 00:10:57.570
it's a societal endeavor, it's a product,

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.281
it's a component of society,

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:01.616
just like my love story.

00:11:03.493 --> 00:11:09.457
And so in this, the story of the electron
and the water molecule

00:11:09.457 --> 00:11:13.127
who meet, fall in love,
who produce the green electron,

00:11:13.169 --> 00:11:17.173
who has the power
to reimagine the world's energy system.

00:11:18.216 --> 00:11:22.512
I believe that they and therefore we

00:11:22.553 --> 00:11:26.599
can live happily ever after,

00:11:26.641 --> 00:11:29.602
but only, as in the marriage ceremony,

00:11:29.644 --> 00:11:32.605
only with the support
of their family and friends.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:37.610
In the UK, the traditional
response to the question,

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:41.197
"Will you support and uphold the newlyweds
now and in the years to come,"

00:11:41.197 --> 00:11:43.157
is, "We will."

00:11:44.992 --> 00:11:46.410
And for our lovers?

00:11:48.037 --> 00:11:49.288
Will we?

00:11:50.164 --> 00:11:52.124
Their family and friends?

00:11:52.124 --> 00:11:54.168
Government? Industry?

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:57.838
Society? Consumers? Voters?

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:00.883
Will we support and uphold them?

00:12:01.592 --> 00:12:03.427
We need to.

00:12:03.469 --> 00:12:06.389
We must. We will.

00:12:06.389 --> 00:12:07.640
Thank you.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:12.603
(Applause)

